{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfPkdufGt3xPQjAQVgEEE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Web search permission wasn't granted, so I could not verify anything online. That changes what I can responsibly hand you. Below is everything from my own knowledge, sorted by how much weight it will bear — because a fabricated citation under your byline is worse than a thin fact sheet, and citation details (exact years, publishers, page-level attributions) are precisely what I'd get subtly wrong.\n\n**Grant web access and I'll verify the whole sheet and hand you a clean, print-ready version.** Until then, treat Tier B and C as leads, not sources.\n\n---\n\n## Tier A — I'd stand behind these\n\n**The primary period source**\n- **Caius, John.** *Of Englishe Dogges: The Diuersities, the Names, the Natures, and the Properties.* Trans. Abraham Fleming. London: Richard Jones, 1576. (Translation of *De Canibus Britannicis*, 1570.)\n- This is the earliest substantial English description of the turnspit. Caius files it among the low-status \"mungrell and rascall\" curs — the turnspit was never a gentleman's dog, which is part of why it vanished without much lament.\n- The passage everyone quotes runs approximately: *\"when any meate is to bee roasted they go into a wheele which they turning about with the waight of their bodies, so diligently looke to their businesse, that no drudge nor skullion can doe the feate more cunningly.\"* **Collate this against a specific edition before printing it as a quotation** — spelling and lineation vary between the 1576 original and later reprints.\n\n**The standard natural-history entries**\n- **Bewick, Thomas.** *A General History of Quadrupeds.* Newcastle upon Tyne: Beilby & Bewick, 1790. Contains a \"Turnspit\" entry with Bewick's woodcut — the most reproduced period image of the animal.\n- **Jesse, Edward.** *Anecdotes of Dogs.* London: Richard Bentley, 1846. The richest 19th-century anecdotal source, and the origin of the much-repeated Bath Abbey story (dogs bolting from the church when a visiting preacher said \"it was then that Ezekiel saw the *wheel*\").\n\n**The physical facts**\n- Long-bodied, short and often bowed legs — bred for the wheel, not the other way around. Worked a treadwheel mounted on the kitchen wall, geared to the roasting spit.\n- Commonly kept in pairs and worked in alternating shifts, which is why the animal appears in period kitchen inventories as equipment as much as livestock.\n\n**A debunk worth including**\n- The claim that \"every dog has his day\" comes from turnspit shift-rotation is **folk etymology**. The phrase predates the usage: Shakespeare, *Hamlet* (Act V, sc. i) — \"The cat will mew and dog will have his day.\" Same for the claim that turnspits inspired \"cur.\" Both circulate widely in popular write-ups; neither holds.\n\n---\n\n## Tier B — real, but verify the bibliographic details\n\n- **Bondeson, Jan.** *Amazing Dogs: A Cabinet of Canine Curiosities.* I'm confident Bondeson has a substantial turnspit chapter and is a credible modern secondary source. **Publisher and year need checking** — I have competing recollections (Cornell UP vs. Amberley, c. 2011).\n- **\"Whiskey\"** — a taxidermied turnspit dog held at **Abergavenny Museum**, Monmouthshire, Wales. The standard surviving physical specimen. Verify the spelling, the museum's current attribution, and whether the museum describes the animal as an actual working dog or a late pet — popular accounts blur this.\n- **Mechanical displacement**: the clockwork/smoke jack made the dog redundant across the first half of the 19th century. Solid as a general cause; I can't cite you a specific technical history from memory.\n- **United States**: the practice's end is routinely tied to early ASPCA-era animal-welfare pressure (ASPCA founded 1866, Henry Bergh). Directionally right; the specific causal claim needs a source.\n\n---\n\n## Tier C — the thing you actually asked for, and I can't give it to you\n\n**\"When were the last working ones recorded\"** is the weakest point on this sheet, and I'm not going to manufacture a date.\n\nWhat I can say honestly: the turnspit was rare in British kitchens by the 1850s and is generally described as extinct as a type by around 1900. What I **do not** have is a specific, citable last-recorded-working-dog — a year, a place, a named kitchen, an observer. Popular articles often assert one; I can't reconstruct which assertion traces to a real record versus which is repetition. Any confident single date I produced here would be invention.\n\nIf the piece hangs on that date, it needs a real source before it runs.\n\n---\n\n## Check before print\n1. The Caius quotation, against a named edition.\n2. Bondeson's publisher and year.\n3. Abergavenny's own wording on Whiskey.\n4. Bewick — confirm the turnspit entry is in the 1790 first edition, not added in a later one.\n5. Any \"last recorded\" date you introduce from elsewhere.\n\nSay the word on web access and I'll run all five down, add the modern secondary sources (there's solid recent journalism on this I'd want to pull rather than paraphrase from memory), and publish it as a clean page you can send straight to your editor."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":299,"cache_read_input_tokens":32127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":299},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b7400a3-7c27-4903-8c43-c35924900d28","uuid":"e48610d0-7ea3-4636-a21a-bade91fb962d","timestamp":"2026-09-25T07:21:45.686Z","request_id":"req_011CfPkduDzBDc9eZkeJHbSv"}
